REPAIR / SERVICE-ENVELOPE CONTEXT

The pre-freeze mechanical diagnosis already established that some corrupted
Task-2 requests first reach egress at age 30-31 under the inherited
32-epoch expiry.

The accepted Task-1G lineage established the same general boundary:
when corruption becomes observable with insufficient remaining lifetime,
detect -> repair -> mandatory reverify is mathematically impossible
without changing the service envelope.

LU-2G did not change:

expiry;
load;
scheduler;
repair math;
verification math;
corruption schedule;
primary baseline criteria;
recovery criteria;
learned authority.

No post-result tuning occurred.

INTERPRETATION

LU-2G does not answer the intended transfer question cleanly.

The exact parent itself fails the frozen Task-2 baseline contract in
6 of 10 fresh worlds, and the frozen recovery definition is not reached
even in the four baseline-feasible worlds.

Therefore the second-task benchmark is not yet a valid measuring instrument
for deciding whether bounded learned U authority generalizes.

The learned policy did causally change real developmental transitions
and did not show an obvious throughput or correctness collapse,
but those observations are not sufficient for a positive transfer claim.

NEXT SCIENTIFIC UNCERTAINTY

Which component of the frozen Task-2 service envelope makes the exact parent
baseline-infeasible:

A. doubled PROCESS path length;
B. offered load of three requests per epoch;
C. inherited 32-epoch expiry relative to the longer pipeline;
D. the recovery target definition calibrated from Task-1 rather than Task-2;
E. interaction among these factors?

NEXT JUSTIFIED STEP

Run an observation-only service-envelope diagnosis on the exact ten bound
LU-2G U_A0 worlds.

Do not alter worlds or dynamics.

Measure:

pre-SENSE wait;
SENSE -> PROCESS_A wait;
PROCESS_A -> PROCESS_B wait;
PROCESS_B -> first ROUTE wait;
route-hop waits;
last ROUTE -> first VERIFY wait;
age and remaining lifetime at first VERIFY;
deadline-impossible corruption count;
finite-horizon censoring;
phase-specific completion capacity;
maximum achievable four-epoch recovery rate under observed service;
and whether the null recovery values arise because the target itself is
unreachable for the exact parent.

Use that diagnosis to preregister a new baseline-calibrated Task-2 environment.

Do not rewrite LU-2G or reinterpret it as qualified.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

The neural part did not obviously fail.

The exact hand-written parent failed our new Task-2 measuring stick
in six of ten worlds before the neural comparison could count.

Even in the four worlds that passed the baseline screen,
the recovery target was never reached.

So the right conclusion is not
"the learned cell cannot transfer."

It is:

"this second-task benchmark is too badly calibrated to tell us yet."

The neural U decisions still changed 58 real cell transitions,
overall throughput stayed very close to the exact parent,
and neither arm produced a wrong completed answer.

Now we need to measure where the Task-2 benchmark is consuming its service
budget and build the next Task-2 contract around what the exact parent can
actually do.

